---
description: Enforce version branches, numbered builds, local testing, and deployment approval
alwaysApply: true
---

# Development and Deployment Cycle

1. `main` is the single source of truth for the latest approved, deploy-authorized
   build. Ring state (which version each of `alpha`, `beta`, `stable`,
   `production` is offered) lives on the updates server, not in the repo. Never
   develop directly on `main`.
2. Start each release from current `main` on
   `version/MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` (for example, `version/1.1.0`). Before cutting the
   branch, confirm that the branch the fleet currently runs is fully contained in
   `main`: `git log --oneline main..<fleet branch>` must print nothing. A fix that
   lives only on a side branch is a regression waiting for the next release.
3. Number every release artifact build
   `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.BUILD`, beginning with `.1` and incrementing for every
   rebuilt candidate. Never replace an artifact under an existing build number.
4. Before production, run the relevant local formatting, lint, test, build,
   upgrade, rollback, and smoke checks. Record the exact commit and build number
   that passed. A deployable candidate must come from a clean committed tree;
   a build marked `dirty` is diagnostic only and cannot be deployed.
5. After local validation, stop and report the results. Do not merge to `main`,
   push a production release, upload artifacts, restart production services, or
   deploy while waiting.
6. Deploy to `updates.mysoc.ai` only after the user gives an explicit deployment
   instruction. Deploy only the exact tested build. Order: the updates server
   first (if it changed), then publish the updater artifacts to the approved
   ring with `scripts/publish-updater.sh` (target groups are mandatory). The
   ring self-updates in any order; every change must tolerate any version mix
   across relay hops.
7. Deployed means tagged. When a build is deployed to any ring, merge the version
   branch to `main` (fast-forward) and tag the exact build commit
   `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.BUILD`. Any material change after testing invalidates
   approval and restarts the build-and-test cycle with a new build number.
8. Widening a build to a further ring needs the updater soak defined in
   `docs/UPDATER-RELEASE.md` to pass on the previous ring and a separate explicit
   instruction per ring.
